Yield the HTML parser every 100 ms instead of 500 ms
(WEBKIT_PARSER_TIME_LIMIT_MS, default 100; =0 restores stock 500).

During the parse phase of a page load the parser pumps tokens for up to
500 ms before yielding to the run loop; queued touch events can only be
serviced in those yields, so the page feels frozen in 500 ms steps on a
phone. Cut the slice to 100 ms so input gets a service window 5x more
often. Each yield is a single zero-delay run-loop turn, so parse
throughput is essentially unchanged. Pairs with
webkit-loading-timer-alignment-env.patch.

--- a/Source/WebCore/html/parser/HTMLParserScheduler.cpp	2026-07-05 17:04:47.847899780 +0000
+++ b/Source/WebCore/html/parser/HTMLParserScheduler.cpp	2026-07-05 17:05:52.735200038 +0000
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@
 #include "LocalFrameView.h"
 #include "ScriptController.h"
 #include "ScriptElement.h"
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <wtf/TZoneMallocInlines.h>
 #include "FrameDestructionObserverInlines.h"
 
@@ -44,8 +45,18 @@
 
 static Seconds parserTimeLimit(Page* page)
 {
-    // Always yield after exceeding this.
-    constexpr auto defaultParserTimeLimit = 500_ms;
+    // Always yield after exceeding this. The stock 500 ms slice leaves the
+    // main thread input-dead for up to half a second at a time during the
+    // HTML-parsing phase of a page load; on a phone that reads as a frozen,
+    // unscrollable page. Yield more often so queued touch events get
+    // serviced between parser chunks -- each yield is just one run-loop
+    // turn, so parse throughput barely changes.
+    // WEBKIT_PARSER_TIME_LIMIT_MS overrides (default 100; 0 = stock 500).
+    static const Seconds defaultParserTimeLimit = []() -> Seconds {
+        const char* env = getenv("WEBKIT_PARSER_TIME_LIMIT_MS");
+        double ms = env ? atof(env) : 100.0;
+        return ms > 0 ? Seconds::fromMilliseconds(ms) : Seconds::fromMilliseconds(500);
+    }();
 
     // We're using the poorly named customHTMLTokenizerTimeDelay setting.
     if (page && page->hasCustomHTMLTokenizerTimeDelay())
